Iterating collections.

Every collection exists to be walked over. Java gives you several ways, and the differences matter — one of them has a famous trap. The unifying idea is that any collection is iterable, so it works with all the tools built on that interface.

The everyday loop is the enhanced for loop, and it's your default. It reads cleanly, and works on any iterable — every list, set, and queue. For each name in names, print the name. Under the hood, the compiler turns this into calls on an iterator — a little cursor with two methods, has-next and next. You rarely name the iterator yourself, but knowing it's there explains the trap that's coming.

And here's the trap. Change a collection's structure — that is, add or remove — while a for-each loop is walking it, and you get a concurrent-modification exception. So if you loop over names, and inside the loop call names dot remove when a name is blank, it throws. The iterator notices that the collection changed underneath it, and it fails fast — deliberately, so that a subtle bug becomes a loud crash. And despite the name, this happens on a single thread; it's about structural modification, not about concurrency.

There are three correct fixes. The cleanest is remove-if, which is purpose-built for exactly this: names dot remove-if, with a predicate that a name is blank. One line, no exception. The second is to use an explicit iterator, and call remove on the iterator itself, which it permits — so you walk with has-next and next, and when the current element is blank, you call it dot remove, safely. The third is collect-then-remove: gather the doomed elements in one pass, then remove them in another. For maps, the same rule applies — mutate through the entry-set iterator, or use entry-set dot remove-if, or values dot remove-if.

Every collection also has a for-each method that takes a lambda, which is handy for a quick side-effect. Names dot for-each, printing each one. Or on a map, for-each with a lambda of name and age. It's concise, but it's still a loop for effects. When you want to transform, filter, or aggregate, rather than just visit, that's the job of streams — names dot stream, filter, map, to-list — which get their own module, module seven. So think of for-each as the functional twin of the for-each loop, and streams as the step beyond.

So, choosing. Reach for the for-each loop by default — it's the clearest way to visit every element. Use remove-if, or an explicit iterator, the moment you need to delete during a walk. Use the for-each method for a tidy one-liner side-effect. And when the loop is really building a new result from the old, remember that streams are waiting in module seven. Next, a different concern that shapes how you hand out collections: making them immutable, and defending the mutable ones.
